Template for projects/<project>/<harness>/Statements.v.  Prose blocks are one
paragraph per line; `oft generate` wraps them (render.wrap).  Every value
comes from harness.yaml; the Rocq terms come from tools/oft/shapes.py.

@@ file
(** * $project / $harness: theorem statements

$header *)

From Stdlib Require Import QArith.
From OssFuzzTheorems.Core Require Import Shapes.
From $rocq Require Images$distribution_require.
$sections
(** ** Proving a statement

$proving *)

@@ header
SUBJECT.  $subject, static riscv64 Linux programs run as [<file> FILE]:
$build_table
[Images] names their memory images.  $driver

WHAT THE HARNESS CHECKS.  $checks

Whether each statement is open, proved or known false is recorded in the harness README, not here: this file changes only when a statement does.

@@ subject_oss-fuzz
$count builds of OSS-Fuzz's [$harness] harness for $library

@@ subject_upstream
$count builds of $project's own fuzz target [$harness], as OSS-Fuzz builds it, for $library

@@ subject_this-repository
$count builds of the API driver written for this collection, over $library

@@ build_plain
  plain       no sanitizer
@@ build_ubsan
  ubsan       UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer; a failed check executes a trap
@@ build_asan
  asan        AddressSanitizer; a report aborts
@@ build_asan-ubsan
  asan_ubsan  both

@@ driver_harness
A driver linked into every build reads FILE as [u16 config word][config bytes][u32 allocation mask][payload] (little-endian; the low 15 bits of the config word are the config length, and this harness binary ignores the mask), calls $entry(payload, length) one to four times ((config[0] & 3) + 1; once when there is no config), and prints each return value.  With a wrong argument count, a missing or unreadable FILE, a FILE shorter than its header, or one of 64 MiB or more, it exits with status 2 without calling the harness.  Every statement quantifies over all of these cases and over every other process input (see Vocabulary in OssFuzzTheorems.Core.Shapes).

@@ driver_api
A driver linked into every build reads FILE as [u16 config word][config bytes][u32 allocation mask][payload] (little-endian; the low 15 bits of the config word are the config length) and runs the payload as a program of calls into $library_name's API ($source); a set bit of the allocation mask makes the corresponding allocation fail.  With a wrong argument count, a missing or unreadable FILE, a FILE shorter than its header, or one of 64 MiB or more, it exits with status 2 without running anything.  Every statement quantifies over all of these cases and over every other process input (see Vocabulary in OssFuzzTheorems.Core.Shapes).

@@ checks_some
$summary  The $who checks:
$items
$live_note

@@ check_item
($letter) $what [$stops; $live]

@@ checks_none
$summary  The $who makes no check of its own beyond not crashing, so a statement about it says only that the code it calls $claim.

@@ live_all
Each check was confirmed in the binary (its assertion text or message is in the binary's bytes), so a failed one ends the process as marked.

@@ live_some
Only the checks marked "confirmed in the binary" had their assertion text or message found in the binary's bytes; for the others no usable text was found, which does not mean that they are compiled out, and no statement below relies on them.

@@ section
(** ** $title *)

@@ definition
(** $doc *)
Definition $name$params : Prop :=
  $body.

@@ not_rendered
(* $name: $status; see README.md. *)

@@ title_halts
Halting
@@ title_agreement
Agreement of each sanitizer build with the plain build
@@ title_bound
How often the builds disagree
@@ title_decidability
Deciding where the builds agree

@@ doc_halts_budget
On every input on which the $build build is never refused memory for exceeding $budget, and when the environment refuses nothing it is not forced to, every execution of the $build build exits with $exits.  It never crashes, fails an assertion, hangs, or leaves the model.$binds$note

Not claimed: anything about what the program writes; anything about other compilers, architectures or $library_name commits.

@@ doc_halts_may_fail
Every execution of the $build build exits, with $exits, on every input and whatever the environment refuses (any allocation, open or read may fail at any time).$stronger$binds$note$no_asan

Not claimed: anything about what the program writes.

@@ no_asan
  There is no ASan statement of this form: the ASan runtime deliberately waits forever when its start-up reservation is refused, so such a statement would be false for a reason that has nothing to do with this $who.

@@ stronger
  This is stronger than [$budget_name].

@@ binds_one
  Check $letters ends the process when it fails, which this statement excludes, so a proof establishes it for the code in this binary on every input it covers.
@@ binds_many
  Checks $letters each end the process when they fail, which this statement excludes, so a proof establishes them for the code in this binary on every input it covers.

@@ note_plain

@@ note_ubsan
  A failed UBSan check traps, so a proof also shows that no check UBSan makes in this build fails on any such input.
@@ note_asan
  An ASan report aborts, so a proof also shows that ASan detects no memory error, in the code it instruments, on any such input (the harness README lists the code this build links uninstrumented).  The runtime's own address-space reservations are not charged to the budget.
@@ note_asan-ubsan
  An ASan report aborts and a failed UBSan check traps, so a proof also shows that neither sanitizer fires, in the code they instrument, on any such input (the harness README lists the code this build links uninstrumented).

@@ doc_agreement
On every input on which the plain build is never refused memory for exceeding $plain_budget, $environment: neither build ever faults, aborts or leaves the model, and the two write the same bytes and exit with the same status.  $san_note$variant

Not claimed: anything about inputs on which the plain build needs more than $plain_budget$not_claimed.

@@ env_may-fail
whatever the environment refuses in either build
@@ env_alloc-forced-only
when a memory request is refused only if the budget is spent or no address range is free (any other request may still be refused, in either build)

@@ agree_ubsan
A failed UBSan check traps, so a proof shows that no UBSan check in this build fails on any such input.
@@ agree_asan
An ASan report aborts, so a proof shows that ASan reports nothing on any such input: no memory error that this build's ASan detects happens in the code it instruments (the harness, $library_name and the driver, less the code the harness README lists as uninstrumented).
@@ agree_asan-ubsan
An ASan report aborts and a failed UBSan check traps, so a proof shows that neither sanitizer fires on any such input in the code they instrument (the harness, $library_name and the driver, less the code the harness README lists as uninstrumented).

@@ not_claimed_asan
; memory errors ASan does not detect (for example an access that lands inside another live object, or one made in code it does not instrument)

@@ variant_abort
  Stated with abort() read as death by SIGABRT rather than as a step the model does not cover; the statement still forbids it.
@@ variant_rv64gc
  The process is told that its machine is RV64GC (no vector unit).

@@ doc_bound
For an input drawn from [Distribution.w] (Distribution.v), the probability that the plain and $san builds are not both error-free with equal traces is at most [b].  Agreement here is judged at 16 GiB / 512 GiB with no budget premise (Core.Shapes), so [$agrees] does not by itself give [$name 0].  Smaller [b] is stronger; [b = 1] holds trivially.

@@ doc_decidability
Some well-formed riscv64 program decides exactly the finite inputs on which the plain and $san builds agree ([Characterise.v] states the problem for a given program).

@@ proving
A proof is a directory proofs/$project/$harness/<statement>/ whose Proof.v ends, for example, in
  Theorem ${example}_holds : Statements.$example.
$bound_example
See docs/proving.md.

@@ bound_example
A bound proof names its bound:
  Theorem ${name}_holds : Statements.$name (1 # 1000).

